\begin_layout Title
Macroeconomic Theory I: Homework 6
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*

\bar under
Problem 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Trees in the utility function.
 Consider the following version of the Lucas Tree model.
 there are two kinds of trees.
 One kind is beautiful and yields utility.
 The other kind is ugly and yields no utility.
 Both trees yield an identical stream of identical fruit.
 The beautiful tree dividends at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{1t}$
\end_inset

, and ugly tree dividends at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{2t}$
\end_inset

.
 Further, 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{1t}=d_{2t}=\frac{d_{t}}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the physical output of the two trees are identical at each point
 in time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The fruite itself is non-storable and generates utility.
 Trees last forever, and trees are the only source of fruit.
 There is no production in the economy, other than fruit from trees.
 There is one of each kind of tree for each individual in the economy.
 The inital allocation of tree is that each individual owns one beautiful
 and one ugly tree.
 There are 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 individuals in the economy.
 Assume the dividend process is such that 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{t}[d_{t+j}]=d_{t}$
\end_inset

, for any 
\begin_inset Formula $j>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consumers are identical, so we can work with a representative consumer.
 Preferences are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{0}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{t}[\ln(c_{t})+\gamma\ln(s_{1t})],\ \gamma>0}
\]

\end_inset

Here 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}$
\end_inset

 is consumption in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{1t}$
\end_inset

 is shares of beautiful trees at the beginning of time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{it}$
\end_inset

 be the price of shares of a tree of type 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 during period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{it}$
\end_inset

 be the gross return on trees of type 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 held from 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

.
 Consider an equilibrium of this economy with markets in stocks of each
 type of tree.
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(a) Set up the household maximization problem as a dynamic programming problem.
 Derive the pricing funciton mapping the state of the economy at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 into price 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2t}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The household's maximization problem is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
 & {\displaystyle \max}\ E_{0}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{t}[\ln(c_{t})+\gamma\ln(s_{1t})]}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & c_{t}+p_{1,t}s_{1,t+1}+p_{2,t}s_{2,t+1}\leq(d_{1t}+p_{1t})s_{1t}+(d_{2t}+p_{2t})s_{2t}\\
 & s_{10}=s_{20}=1
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

A competitive equilibrium is given by: a sequence of allocations 
\begin_inset Formula $\{c_{t},s_{1t},s_{2t}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 and prices 
\begin_inset Formula $\{p_{1t},p_{2t}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 that solves the household's problem and also satisfies the market clearing
 conditions.
 Since the households are homogeneous, no trading occurs in equilibrium.
 Accordingly, the market clearing conditions gives us:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s_{i0}=1=s_{it}\ \forall\ t\in\mathbb{N},\ i=1,2
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c_{t}=d_{t}\ \forall\ t\in\mathbb{N}
\]

\end_inset

We can set up the household maximization problem as a dynamic programming
 problem.
 The Bellman Equation is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
V(s_{1},s_{2},d) & = & {\displaystyle \max_{s_{1}^{\prime},s_{2}^{\prime}}\{\ln(c)+\gamma\ln(s_{1})+\beta E[V(s_{1}^{\prime},s_{2}^{\prime},d)]}\}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{where}\ c=(d_{1}+p_{1})s_{1}+(d_{2}+p_{2})s_{2}-p_{1}s_{1}^{\prime}-p_{2}s_{2}^{\prime}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first order condition of the right hand side of the Bellman Equation
 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{1}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-\frac{p_{1}}{c}+\beta E\left[\frac{d_{1}^{\prime}+p_{1}^{\prime}}{c^{\prime}}+\frac{\gamma}{s_{1}^{\prime}}\right]=0
\]

\end_inset

In equilibrium, no trading occurs, so 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{1}^{\prime}=1\ \mbox{and}\ d_{1}^{\prime}=\frac{d^{\prime}}{2}=\frac{c^{\prime}}{2}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\implies\frac{p_{1}}{d}=\frac{\beta}{2}+\beta\gamma+\beta E\left[\frac{p_{1}^{\prime}}{d^{\prime}}\right]
\]

\end_inset

Now, let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}=\frac{p_{1}}{d},\ x_{t+1}=\frac{p_{1}^{\prime}}{d^{\prime}},\ A=\frac{\beta}{2}+\beta\gamma$
\end_inset

.
 So, the equation above can be rewritten as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{t} & = & A+\beta E(x_{t+1})\ \forall\ t\\
 & = & A+\beta E\left[A+\beta(x_{t+2})\right]\\
 & \vdots\\
x_{t} & = & A+\beta E\left[D+\beta^{s-1}(x_{t+s})\right]\ \ \mbox{where \ensuremath{D}\ is a constant}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta^{s-1}\rightarrow0\ \mbox{as}\ s\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}=A+\beta D$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}$
\end_inset

 is a linear function of 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is a linear function of 
\begin_inset Formula $d^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 As a result,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E_{t}[d_{t+j}] & = & d_{t}\ \forall\ j>0\\
 & \implies & E\left[\frac{p_{1}^{\prime}}{d^{\prime}}\right]=\frac{p_{1}}{d}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{p_{1}}{d} & = & \frac{\beta}{2}+\beta\gamma+\beta\frac{p_{1}}{d}\\
p_{1}(d) & = & \frac{(1+2\gamma)\beta d}{2(1-\beta)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The first order condition of the right hand side of the Bellman Equation
 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{2}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-\frac{p_{2}}{c}+\beta E\left[\frac{d_{2}^{\prime}+p_{2}^{\prime}}{c^{\prime}}\right]=0
\]

\end_inset

In equilibrium, 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{2}^{\prime}=\frac{d^{\prime}}{2}=\frac{c^{\prime}}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Also, using the same argument from before, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is a linear function of 
\begin_inset Formula $d^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Hence,
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\frac{p_{2}^{\prime}}{d^{\prime}}\right]=\frac{p_{2}}{d}$
\end_inset

.
 We now have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{p_{2}}{d} & = & \frac{\beta}{2}+\beta\frac{p_{2}}{d}\\
p_{2}(d) & = & \frac{\beta d}{2(1-\beta)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(b) Show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma>0$
\end_inset

 is sufficient for 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{1t}<R_{2t}$
\end_inset

.
 That is, beautiful trees are dominated in rate of return.
 Also, explain your result intuitively.
\series default

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
R_{1t} & = & \frac{p_{1,t+1}+d_{1,t+1}}{p_{1t}}\\
 & = & \left(1+\frac{2(1-\beta)}{(1+2\gamma)\beta}\right)\frac{d_{t+1}}{d_{t}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
R_{2t} & = & \frac{p_{1,t+1}+d_{1,t+1}}{p_{1t}}\\
 & = & \left(1+\frac{2(1-\beta)}{\beta}\right)\frac{d_{t+1}}{d_{t}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
R_{2t}-R_{1t} & = & \left(\frac{2(1-\beta)}{\beta}-\frac{2(1-\beta)}{(1+2\gamma)\beta}\right)\frac{d_{t+1}}{d_{t}}\\
 & = & \left(1-\frac{1}{1+2\gamma}\right)\frac{2(1-\beta)}{\beta}\frac{d_{t+1}}{d_{t}}>0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $R_{2t}>R_{1t}$
\end_inset

 means the ugly trees have a higher rate of return.
 The beautiful trees have a higher price because they provide utility to
 households, but they produce the same amount of fruit as an ugly tree.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*

\bar under
Problem 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider an economy with a continuum of identical consumers.
 The utility of a representatitive consumer is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{t}u(c_{t})}\right]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}$
\end_inset

 is consumption and 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 is the expectation operator.
 Each consumer owns 2 trees, indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

.
 The fruit of tree 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\{g,b\},y_{it},$
\end_inset

 evolves as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{t}^{i}=\begin{cases}
\theta_{t} & i=b\\
1-\theta_{t} & i=g
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{t}$
\end_inset

 is an aggregate shock whose value is either 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $0<L<H<1$
\end_inset

.
 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{t}$
\end_inset

 follows a two-state Markov chain with
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{c}
\mbox{Pr}[\theta_{t+1}=H|\theta_{t}=H]=\pi,\ \mbox{Pr}[\theta_{t+1}=L|\theta_{t}=H]=1-\pi\\
\mbox{Pr}[\theta_{t+1}=H|\theta_{t}=L]=1-\pi,\ \mbox{Pr}[\theta_{t+1}=L|\theta_{t}=L]=\pi
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

There is one share in each tree and these shares are competitively traded.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{it}$
\end_inset

 be the price of tree 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\{g,b\}$
\end_inset

 in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 In addition to shares, there is also a riskless one-period bond.
 A bond sold in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is a claim to one unti of consumption (i.e., fruit) in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t}$
\end_inset

 be the price of a bond in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(a) Solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}^{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t}$
\end_inset

 in each aggregate state.
 Also determine the real interest rate in each state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The maximization problem for the representative consumer is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
 & {\displaystyle \max}\ E_{0}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{t}[u(c_{t})]}\right]\\
\mbox{s.t.} & c_{t}+p_{t}^{b}s_{t+1}^{b}+p_{t}^{g}s_{t+1}^{g}+q_{t}b_{t+1}\leq y_{t}^{b}s_{t}^{b}+y_{t}^{g}s_{t}^{g}+p_{t}^{b}s_{t}^{b}+p_{t}^{g}s_{t}^{g}+b_{t}\\
 & s_{0}^{b}=s_{0}^{g}=1
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A competitive equilibrium is given by: a sequence of allocations 
\begin_inset Formula $\{c_{t},s_{t}^{b},s_{t}^{g}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 and prices 
\begin_inset Formula $\{p_{t}^{b},p_{t}^{g}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 that solves the household's problem and also satisfies the market clearing
 conditions.
 Since the households are homogeneous, no trading occurs in equilibrium.
 Also, consumption stays constant since aggregate endowment is constant
 across time and all agents are homogenous.
 Accordingly, the market clearing conditions gives us:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s_{0}^{i}=1=s_{t}^{i}\ \forall\ t\in\mathbb{N},\ i\in\{b,g\}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c_{t}=1\ \forall\ t\in\mathbb{N}
\]

\end_inset

To determine 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t}$
\end_inset

, we obtain the first order condition of the maximization problem with resptect
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{t+1}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\partial\beta^{t}u(c_{t})}{\partial b_{t+1}}+\frac{\partial\beta^{t}u(c_{t+1})}{\partial b_{t+1}} & = & 0\\
-\beta^{t}u^{\prime}(c_{t})q_{t}+\beta^{t+1}u(c_{t+1}) & = & 0\\
q_{t} & = & \beta\frac{u^{\prime}(c_{t+1})}{u^{\prime}(c_{t})}\\
q_{t} & = & \beta\ \ \mbox{since \ensuremath{u^{\prime}(c_{t})=u^{\prime}(c_{t+1})}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the interest rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{t}$
\end_inset

, is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r_{t}=\frac{1}{q_{t}}
\]

\end_inset

To calculate the asset-pricing function for tree 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, we obtain the first order condition of the maximization problem with respect
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t+1}^{i}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
-\beta^{t}u^{\prime}(c_{t})p_{t}^{i}+\beta^{t+1}u^{\prime}(c_{t+1})E_{t}\left[y_{t+1}^{i}+p_{t+1}^{i}\right] & = & 0\\
u^{\prime}(c_{t})p_{t}^{i} & = & \beta E_{t}\left[y_{t+1}^{i}+p_{t+1}^{i}\right]u^{\prime}(c_{t+1})\\
p_{t}^{i} & = & \beta E_{t}(p_{t+1}^{i})+\beta E_{t}(y_{t+1}^{i})\ \ \mbox{(since }u^{\prime}(c_{t})=u^{\prime}(c_{t+1})\mbox{)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Note,
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{b}$
\end_inset

 only takes on two possible values, 
\begin_inset Formula $H\ \mbox{and}\ L$
\end_inset

, and it evolves through time according to a Markov chain.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}^{b}$
\end_inset

 only depends on the current state, and not the entire history.
 As a result, we can express the price of tree 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 as a function of the state at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}^{b}=p^{b}(\sigma)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma\in\{H,L\}$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p^{b}(H) & = & \beta\left[\pi p^{b}(H)+(1-\pi)p^{b}(L)\right]+\beta\left[\pi H+(1-\pi)L\right]\\
p^{b}(L) & = & \beta\left[(1-\pi)p^{b}(H)+\pi p^{b}(L)\right]+\beta[(1-\pi)H+\pi L]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

These two equations can be written succinctly in matrix form as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left[\begin{array}{c}
p^{b}(H)\\
p^{b}(L)
\end{array}\right] & = & \beta\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\pi & (1-\pi)\\
(1-\pi) & \pi
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
p^{b}(H)\\
p^{b}(L)
\end{array}\right]+\beta\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\pi & (1-\pi)\\
(1-\pi) & \pi
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
H\\
L
\end{array}\right]\\
p^{b} & = & \beta Pp^{b}+\beta Pv
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The equation can be represented as 
\begin_inset Formula $(I-\beta P)p^{b}=\beta Pv$
\end_inset

.
 This equation has a unique solution given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p^{b}=(I-\beta P)^{-1}\left(\beta Pv\right)
\]

\end_inset

The price of tree 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 in state 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 can be found from the column vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{b}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p^{b}(H) & = & \frac{1-\pi\beta}{(1-\pi\beta)^{2}-\beta^{2}(1-\pi)^{2}}\left[\beta\pi H+\beta(1-\pi)L\right]+\frac{\beta(1-\pi)}{(1-\pi\beta)^{2}-\beta^{2}(1-\pi)^{2}}[\beta(1-\pi)H+\beta\pi L]\\
p^{b}(L) & = & \frac{\beta(1-\pi)}{(1-\pi\beta)^{2}-\beta^{2}(1-\pi)^{2}}\left[\beta\pi H+\beta(1-\pi)L\right]+\frac{1-\pi\beta}{(1-\pi\beta)^{2}-\beta^{2}(1-\pi)^{2}}[\beta(1-\pi)H+\beta\pi L]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, lets consider tree 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

.
 Note,
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{g}$
\end_inset

 only takes on two possible values, 
\begin_inset Formula $1-H\ \mbox{and}\ 1-L$
\end_inset

, and it evolves through time according to a Markov chain.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}^{g}$
\end_inset

 only depends on the current state, and not the entire history.
 As a result, we can express the price of tree 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 as a function of the state at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}^{g}=p^{g}(\lambda)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\in\{1-H,1-L\}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p^{g}(1-H) & = & \beta[\pi p^{g}(1-H)+(1-\pi)p^{g}(1-L)]+\beta[\pi(1-H)+(1-\pi)(1-L)]\\
p^{g}(1-L) & = & \beta[(1-\pi)p^{g}(1-H)+\pi p^{g}(1-L)]+\beta[(1-\pi)(1-H)+\pi(1-L)]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

These two equations can be written succinctly in matrix form as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left[\begin{array}{c}
p^{g}(1-H)\\
p^{g}(1-L)
\end{array}\right] & = & \beta\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\pi & (1-\pi)\\
(1-\pi) & \pi
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
p^{b}(1-H)\\
p^{b}(1-L)
\end{array}\right]+\beta\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\pi & (1-\pi)\\
(1-\pi) & \pi
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
1-H\\
1-L
\end{array}\right]\\
p^{g} & = & \beta Pp^{g}+\beta Px
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The price of tree 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 in state 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 can be found from the column vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{g}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p^{g}(1-H) & = & \frac{1-\pi\beta}{(1-\pi\beta)^{2}-\beta^{2}(1-\pi)^{2}}\left[\beta\pi(1-H)+\beta(1-\pi)(1-L)\right]\\
 &  & +\frac{\beta(1-\pi)}{(1-\pi\beta)^{2}-\beta^{2}(1-\pi)^{2}}[\beta(1-\pi)(1-H)+\beta\pi(1-L)]\\
p^{g}(1-L) & = & \frac{\beta(1-\pi)}{(1-\pi\beta)^{2}-\beta^{2}(1-\pi)^{2}}\left[\beta\pi(1-H)+\beta(1-\pi)(1-L)\right]\\
 &  & +\frac{1-\pi\beta}{(1-\pi\beta)^{2}-\beta^{2}(1-\pi)^{2}}[\beta(1-\pi)(1-H)+\beta\pi(1-L)]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(b) Recall that the risk premium is the difference in rates of return between
 the risky assest and the riskless bond, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{t}\left[\frac{p_{t+1}^{i}+y_{t+1}^{i}}{p_{t}^{i}}-\frac{1}{q_{t}}\right]$
\end_inset

.
 Determine the risk premium for each tree.
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From part (a) above, the first order condition of the maximization problem
 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t+1}^{i}$
\end_inset

 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p_{t}^{i} & = & \beta E_{t}(p_{t+1}^{i}+y_{t+1}^{i})\\
E_{t}\left[\frac{p_{t+1}^{i}+y_{t+1}^{i}}{p_{t}^{i}}\right] & = & \frac{1}{\beta}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E_{t}\left[\frac{p_{t+1}^{i}+y_{t+1}^{i}}{p_{t}^{i}}-\frac{1}{q_{t}}\right] & = & E_{t}\left[\frac{p_{t+1}^{i}+y_{t+1}^{i}}{p_{t}^{i}}\right]-E_{t}\left[\frac{1}{q_{t}}\right]\\
 & = & E_{t}\left[\frac{p_{t+1}^{i}+y_{t+1}^{i}}{p_{t}^{i}}\right]-\frac{1}{q_{t}}\\
 & = & \frac{1}{\beta}-\frac{1}{\beta}\\
 & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

